s, Q of Diamonds, 10 of Clubs, 8 of Hearts.\n\n10: High Card: No made hand; highest single card wins. \n\nNote for Action representation: -1 for Fold, -2 for All-in. 0 for Call/Check. >0 for the amount you raise the bet. \n\nWhen placing a raise, the chips bet must be at least twice the current maximum bet of this round. If no player has bet before in this round, the raise must be at least equal to the big blind.\n\nTo call or raise, a player must have enough chips to cover the required bet. Otherwise, they can only fold or go all-in. An improper bet is considered an illegal action and will be treated as a fold.\n\nNow is your turn, to your best ability, please select an action from your valid actions that maximize your probability of winning. If you have more chips than your opponent by the last hand of the game, you win the game.\nPlease briefly evaluate a few proimsing moves, provide reason for your final choice, and output the action with specified format.\n    Your response format should be:\n    Evaluation: [Your Evaluation]\n    Reason: [Your Reason]\n    Answer: [Your Answer] (should be a number)\n", 'user_prompt': 'This is the 17 hand out of 20 hands. Everyone starts with 20,000 chips each hand, and you have 19950. Your cumulative win/loss on chips until last hand are -700.0, but the cumulative win/loss chips are inaccessible and independent from your current chips (consider them as deposits/debts to a bank).You are currently losing to your opponent.\nYour cards are: 4 of Diamonds, 2 of Spades.\nCurrently there is no public card shown.\nThe Game history is as follows: \nYour valid actions are: [-2 (All-in), -1 (Fold), 0 (Check), 200 (raise the bet), or any value above 200 but lower than your current chips.].\n', 'reasoning': '**My Thoughts on This Texas Hold\'em Hand**\n\nOkay, so I\'m in hand 17 of 20, down 700 chips, with a starting stack of 20,000, and my stack is currently at 19,950. My opponent probably has around 20,050. The cards I\'m holding are a 4 of Diamonds and a 2 of Spades. Yikes. That\'s a *terrible* hand. Honestly, it\'s garbage. Statistically, it\'s one of the worst starting hands you can get. No high cards, not even connected, and unsuited. Pre-flop, the valid actions are All-in, Fold, Check, Raise, and... more than raising.\n\nGiven the option to "Check," I figure I must be in the Big Blind position, and the Small Blind, my opponent, has limped. Okay, I\'m the Big Blind. Good. No point in betting here. My opponent has already invested something, and folding wouldn\'t make sense if I can see the flop for free, meaning Check is my only real option. A check also allows me to keep my stack intact and potentially catch a lucky flop. I\'m losing the match overall, so I want to get something back here.\n\nI\'m thinking, do I try to bluff and go for it? It\'s a high variance play, and considering that I am losing, this might be a desperate catch up move, but this is a *very* bad hand to do it with. My opponent has position on me, and I don\'t want to build a pot with garbage. No way. Fold (-1) is out, because I can check. A Raise (200+) is insane.\n\nSo, I\'m going to check. Let\'s see what the flop brings. Maybe, just maybe, a miracle will happen, and I\'ll hit something that turns this trash into a playable hand. But for now, I\'m playing it safe. No risk, no reward... sometimes that\'s the best option.\n\n**Evaluation:** Check (0)\n**Reason:** My hand (4d 2s) is statistically terrible, and checking is the only logical play given my Big Blind position. It\'s a free look at the flop, and folding pre-flop would be throwing away equity.\n**Answer:** 0\n\n\n', 'output': 'Evaluation: The hand 4 of Diamonds and 2 of Spades is statistically one of the weakest starting hands in Texas Hold\'em due to its low rank, lack of suit connection, and the gap between the cards. However, the game state indicates that "Check (0)" is a valid option. This implies you are the Big Blind and the opponent has merely called the blind amount (limped) rather than raising.\n\nReason: Since checking is free and allows you to see the flop without investing more chips, it is the mathematically correct play. Folding would be a mistake because you can proceed for free. Raising is not recommended with such a weak hand, especially when you are currently losing and need to conserve chips for better opportunities in the remaining hands.\n\nAnswer: 0'}
